        "statement": "Let a local rigid four-dimensional N=1 superspace action datum have the form fixed above, including its canonical matter kinetic term, with finite-dimensional unitary matter representations of a fixed compact gauge group, an additional real D-term integrand $G$, a holomorphic gauge-invariant F-term integrand $F(\\Phi,W)$, and a formal Wilsonian cutoff prescription at a nonzero scale $0<\\lambda<\\Lambda_0$. Assume the selected prescription has the local nondynamical-spurion extension specified in the action data and supplies the one-particle-irreducible cutoff vertex functional and a local derivative expansion coefficientwise in formal perturbation theory around the topologically trivial background, as specified above. Let $\\langle\\cdot\\rangle_\\lambda$ be the normalized coefficientwise expectation of the selected regulated formal expansion. Assume its rigid N=1 and BRST changes of variables obey $\\langle\\delta_{\\epsilon,\\bar\\epsilon}\\mathcal O\\rangle_\\lambda=\\langle\\mathcal O\\,\\delta_{\\epsilon,\\bar\\epsilon}S_\\lambda\\rangle_\\lambda$ and $\\langle s\\mathcal O\\rangle_\\lambda=0$ for every local insertion $\\mathcal O$, with counterterms satisfying the same identities and no local gauge anomaly. Then the Wilsonian chiral integrand is unchanged coefficientwise, $F_\\lambda(\\Phi,W)=F(\\Phi,W)$; the holomorphic gauge-coupling parameters may receive one-loop corrections, but no higher-loop corrections. If an unbroken U(1) factor is present, a Fayet–Iliopoulos parameter can receive a correction only at one loop, and that correction is proportional to the sum of the U(1) charges of all left-chiral multiplets, including multiplicities; it vanishes when this charge sum is zero. The theorem imposes no nonrenormalization condition on the additional real D-term integrand $G_\\lambda$, the Kähler data, or coefficients expressed after a scale-dependent chiral-field rescaling that normalizes a positive real quadratic kinetic form. It is only a formal perturbative Wilsonian statement at nonzero cutoff: it does not assert nonperturbative exactness, a result for an arbitrary one-particle-irreducible functional, or existence of a continuum interacting quantum field theory.",
