## Supersymmetric backgrounds, path integrals, and S4/S3 formulas

Definitions needed before using these terms include: the dimension/signature and
orientation of spacetime; spin structure and spinor bundles; the gauge principal
bundle and connection; each matter representation and field statistics; the
off-shell multiplet and auxiliary fields; the R-symmetry and its background
connection; the supercharge(s) and their reality/normalization convention; and
the background Killing-spinor equations. A rigid supersymmetric background is
not merely a curved manifold: it is a metric plus specified background
supergravity/R-symmetry/auxiliary fields admitting a nonzero solution of the
chosen fermion-variation equations. State the spin structure, reality contour,
R-charges and background fields, and explicitly compute the square `Q^2` as the
combination of isometry, gauge, R-symmetry and flavor transformations used in
the localization complex. Do not assume every metric admits such a background.

A partition function must name its field space, action, integration cycle,
gauge quotient/gauge fixing, measure or regulator, boundary conditions, and
parameters. A formal symbol `∫DΦ e^{-S[Φ]}` is not a constructed measure.
The usual localization step adds `t QV` and differentiates in `t`. This is a
formal physics Ward-identity derivation unless the regulated measure is
Q-invariant, the integration cycle has no boundary contribution, the integral
converges sufficiently to differentiate, the gauge/ghost complex is defined,
and the regulator and limit preserve these properties. An anomaly, contour
deformation, noncompact zero-mode direction, or instanton boundary can invalidate
the step. Name each such assumption rather than hiding it in “supersymmetry
protects the answer.”

The first carriers also need explicit definitions for these objects and their
domains: a **rigid limit** (which background supergravity fields are frozen and
which Planck-scale limit is taken); a **supersymmetry complex** (graded fields,
odd operator `Q`, its domain and exact square `Q^2`); a **gauge-fixed fluctuation
operator** (bundles, Sobolev/domain, ghosts and stabilizers); a **one-loop
determinant** (the ratio of specified bosonic/fermionic spectral determinants,
regularization, phase and zero-mode convention); and a **functional-integral
regulator** (finite mode/lattice/cutoff space, measure, contour and removal
map). For physical formulas, state whether fields and couplings are in SI or
natural units and give every suppressed constant. Under the common `ħ=c=1`
choice, a 4D gauge coupling is dimensionless, `a`, masses and `ε_i` have
mass dimension one, the sphere radius `r` has dimension length, and the
instanton fugacity `q` is dimensionless. Restore units explicitly if a result
is compared with an SI observable.

The finite-dimensional theorem and the field-theory formula have different
domains. The rigorous cohomological localization formula applies to a
finite-dimensional compact manifold (or a separately proved compact-support /
proper-pushforward extension) with smooth torus action, equivariant class and
invertible normal Euler classes after localization. A finite-dimensional
supersymmetric integral also needs the explicit cycle, measure, Ward identity
and decay in `thm-finite-dimensional-q-exact-deformation-invariance`. The
continuum field-space version must separately construct the infinite-dimensional
measure, gauge fixing, determinants, zero modes, instanton sectors, convergence
or a regulator-removal limit. None of the local finite-dimensional theorems
proves that a continuum functional integral exists.

For the round S4 example, Pestun's field-theory derivation for specified 4D
`N=2` vector/hyper theories produces the structural expression

`Z_{S^4} = (1/|W|) ∫_{\mathfrak t} da\, e^{-8π^2 r^2(a,a)/g_{YM}^2}\, Z_{1-loop}(ia)\, |Z_{inst}(ia, ε_1=r^{-1}, ε_2=r^{-1}, q)|^2`,

up to the paper's measure, root and coupling conventions. Here `G` is the named
compact gauge group, `T` a chosen maximal torus, `W` its Weyl group, `r` the
sphere radius, `a` the constant Cartan scalar at the saddle, `Z_{1-loop}` the
gauge-fixed fluctuation determinant, and the north/south pole factors count
instantons and anti-instantons in opposite Ω-backgrounds. A carrier would need
to state the exact representation content, masses, coupling, contour and
normalization before asserting a formula. In these conventions `q=e^{2πiτ}` is
the dimensionless instanton parameter for the declared complexified coupling
`τ`; `ia` records the paper's contour/analytic-continuation convention, and
`(a,a)` uses its specified invariant quadratic form. The formula is a source-reported
formal field-theory result in this scaffold, not a proved continuum theorem.
Pestun explicitly says that the noncommutative S4 deformation is not explicitly
constructed in the paper and assumes it is well-defined and agrees with the
Nekrasov R4 deformation near each pole. That is a material condition, not a
minor footnote.

For a 3D `N=2` theory on the round S3, the Kapustin–Willett–Yaakov calculation
reduces its specified superconformal Chern–Simons–matter model to a Cartan
integral of the form

`Z_{S^3} = (1/|W|) ∫_{\mathfrak t} da\, e^{-S_{cl}(a)} Z^{vec}_{1-loop}(a) Z^{matter}_{1-loop}(a;R)`,

where the vector determinant includes the root/Vandermonde factor and the matter
determinant depends on the declared representations and R-charges `R`. Exact
signs, `2π` factors, level quantization, FI terms and contour depend on the
authors' convention and theory; read and reproduce that convention before
making a formula a local item. KWY's cited result is for superconformal
Chern–Simons–matter theories (including their explicit U(N) and ABJM cases),
not every 3D supersymmetric QFT. Again, it is a field-theoretic localization
calculation, not proof of a general continuum measure. The schematic integral
sets the sphere radius to one; when restoring a radius, state the rescaling of
the Cartan scalar and all dimensionful masses/FI parameters. State the integer
Chern–Simons level and the exact R-charge convention of each chiral multiplet.

The mathematical one-loop route should be split into separate obligations:
define the boson/fermion fluctuation complex and gauge-fixing ghosts; prove the
linearized `Q` complex and its symbol/ellipticity or transverse ellipticity;
identify actual zero modes and remove/gauge them with correct stabilizers;
compute its equivariant character/index under explicit hypotheses; derive the
nonzero-mode determinant ratio from a finite regulator or a stated spectral
regularization; check regularization-scale/anomaly terms; and normalize the
orientation/determinant line. Cite the exact `Q^2` action and ensure all
operators commute with the chosen symmetry. Do not infer the determinant from
the index alone.

## Instanton counting

Define an instanton as a connection `A` on a specified principal bundle over
oriented Euclidean four-space (or a compactified four-manifold), with the
chosen sign of `F_A=±*F_A`, finite action and declared framing/gauge behavior.
Define its integer charge using the exact trace representation and coefficient
normalization. For `U(N)` on `R^4`, Nekrasov's ADHM construction uses finite
dimensional data with `V=C^k`, `W=C^N`, maps `B_1,B_2∈End(V)`, `I∈Hom(W,V)`,
`J∈Hom(V,W)`, complex and real moment-map equations and a stability condition;
the quotient is by `U(k)`. Specify the rotation torus `T^2` with weights
`ε_1,ε_2`, the framing torus and the fixed-point character. In the resolved
ADHM model, fixed points are indexed by `N`-tuples of Young diagrams; the
Euler/virtual weights produce the coefficient of the formal instanton series.

The safe mathematical formula is a **defined equivariant pushforward**
`Z_{inst}(a,ε_1,ε_2;q)=Σ_{k≥0}q^k∫^{eq}_{\mathcal M_{k,N}}1`, only after defining
`\mathcal M_{k,N}`, the equivariant coefficient ring, a legitimate pushforward
for this noncompact space, and the regularization/compactification. It may be
only a formal power series in `q`. Nekrasov emphasizes both the UV small-size
and IR noncompactness of instanton moduli and the use of an Ω-background and
noncommutative/FI deformation to control them. These are separate from a proof
that a continuum 4D gauge-theory path integral equals the finite-dimensional
ADHM count.

Complete local proof obligations for the mathematical counting branch are:
construct the ADHM quotient and show its smoothness under the stated stability
parameter; prove its relation to framed ASD/SD connections; compute the
`T^2×T_G` fixed set and tangent weights; prove the appropriate equivariant
localization/pushforward; check compactness/properness or the exact localized
coefficient convention; state the formal/analytic status of the `q`-sum; and
prove independence from any auxiliary resolution only when an actual
comparison theorem is supplied. The existing ordinary ASD index item gives
neither ADHM classification nor the equivariant pushforward.

