      "fact": "F2",
      "source": "def-qftx-nonabelian-brst-complex",
      "source_section": "Definition",
      "quote": "Let $n\\geq 1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be a nonempty open set with coordinates $x^1,\\ldots,x^n$, and let $\\mathfrak g$ be a finite-dimensional real Lie algebra with bilinear alternating bracket $[\\ ,\\ ]_{\\mathfrak g}$ satisfying the Jacobi identity. Let $V=V_{\\bar 0}\\oplus V_{\\bar 1}$ be a finite-dimensional $\\mathbb Z_2$-graded complex vector space, and let $\\rho:\\mathfrak g\\to\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V)$ be an even representation, meaning $\\rho([X,Y]_{\\mathfrak g})=\\rho(X)\\rho(Y)-\\rho(Y)\\rho(X)$ for all $X,Y\\in\\mathfrak g$ and each $\\rho(X)$ preserves the grading of $V$. This is local Lie-algebra data; no global group, principal bundle, action functional, or quantum theory is selected here.\n\nChoose a basis $T_a$ of $\\mathfrak g$ and write $[T_b,T_c]_{\\mathfrak g}=f^a{}_{bc}T_a$. A local gauge potential is a $\\mathfrak g$-valued one-form $A=A_\\mu^aT_a\\,dx^\\mu$ whose component jet generators $A_{\\mu,I}^a$ are even and have ghost number zero. A matter field is a $V$-valued local field $\\psi$ with each component jet generator $\\psi_I^\\alpha$ assigned the parity of its graded component and ghost number zero. The ghost is $c=c^aT_a$, with odd component jet generators $c_I^a$ of ghost number one. The coefficient bracket of homogeneous $\\mathfrak g$-valued fields is $[X,Y]^a=f^a{}_{bc}X^bY^c$, using multiplication in the graded-commutative coefficient algebra; in particular $[c,c]^a=f^a{}_{bc}c^bc^c$ has no factor of $1/2$.\n\nFor a multi-index $I=(I_1,\\ldots,I_n)\\in\\mathbb N^n$, let $\\Phi_I$ denote the corresponding formal jet generator of a field component $\\Phi$. The finite-jet local algebra $\\mathcal F_U$ is $C^\\infty(U)$ tensored with the polynomial algebra in its even jet generators and the exterior algebra in its odd jet generators, where each element uses only finitely many jet generators. The total derivatives $D_\\mu$ act by $D_\\mu f=\\partial_\\mu f$ on $f\\in C^\\infty(U)$ and $D_\\mu\\Phi_I=\\Phi_{I+e_\\mu}$ on every jet generator; the multi-index derivatives commute.\n\nDefine the degree-one BRST operator candidate $s$ on the fields by\n\n$$sA_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu c+[A_\\mu,c],\\qquad sc=-\\frac12[c,c],\\qquad s\\psi=-\\rho(c)\\psi.$$\n\nOn coefficient functions set $sf=0$, and on every jet generator set $s(\\Phi_I)=D_I(s\\Phi)$; extend $s$ to $\\mathcal F_U$ by the odd left-derivation rule $s(FG)=(sF)G+(-1)^{|F|}F(sG)$ for homogeneous $F$, where $|F|$ is total parity. The minimal sector here contains only $A,\\psi,c$ and their jets. Antighosts and Nakanishi–Lautrup variables are not included; adjoining such a nonminimal pair requires separate definitions and a proof before any cohomological triviality claim. The specified data are a BRST complex once $s^2=0$, which is established for these formulas in the following nilpotence lemma.",
      "uses": [
        "1.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "F3",
      "source": "lem-qftx-nonabelian-brst-nilpotence",
      "source_section": "Statement",
      "quote": "Let $n\\geq 1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty and open, let $\\mathfrak g$ be a finite-dimensional real Lie algebra, and let $V=V_{\\bar 0}\\oplus V_{\\bar 1}$ be a finite-dimensional graded complex vector space with an even Lie-algebra representation $\\rho:\\mathfrak g\\to\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V)$. Form the graded-commutative finite-jet algebra over $C^\\infty(U)$ from an even $\\mathfrak g$-valued local one-form $A=A_\\mu^aT_a\\,dx^\\mu$, a ghost $c=c^aT_a$ with odd components of ghost number one, and matter fields $\\psi^\\alpha$ of fixed component parity and ghost number zero. Let $D_\\mu$ be the total derivatives on coefficient functions and formal field jets, and let $s$ be the left odd derivation of ghost number one specified by $sA_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu c+[A_\\mu,c]$, $sc=-\\tfrac12[c,c]$, $s\\psi=-\\rho(c)\\psi$, and $s(D_I\\Phi)=D_I(s\\Phi)$ on every multi-index jet. Then $s^2=0$ on the entire finite-jet algebra and $sD_\\mu=D_\\mu s$ for each $\\mu$.",
      "uses": [
        "1.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "F4",
      "source": "def-qftx-n1-chiral-superfield",
      "source_section": "Definition",
      "quote": "Let $\\mathcal A=\\mathcal A_{\\bar0}\\oplus\\mathcal A_{\\bar1}$ be the finite-dimensional supercommutative complex coefficient algebra, and let $\\Lambda_4$ have ordered generators $\\theta^1,\\theta^2,\\bar\\theta^{\\dot1},\\bar\\theta^{\\dot2}$ and the product, parity, derivatives and conjugation specified in the four-dimensional superspace Grassmann algebra. Let $\\mathscr S=C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^{1,3},\\mathcal A\\otimes\\Lambda_4)$ be the space of smooth coefficient functions; $\\partial_\\mu$ differentiates the real coordinate $x^\\mu$ and acts trivially on the finite Grassmann basis. Use the mostly-plus metric and the matrices $\\sigma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\dot\\alpha}$ specified by the four-dimensional super-Poincare algebra. A **chiral superfield** is an even element $\\Phi\\in\\mathscr S$ satisfying the two equations\n\n$$\\bar D_{\\dot\\alpha}\\Phi=0,\\qquad \\bar D_{\\dot\\alpha}:=-\\partial^L_{\\bar\\theta^{\\dot\\alpha}}-i\\sum_{\\alpha,\\mu}\\theta^\\alpha\\sigma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\dot\\alpha}\\partial_\\mu,\\quad \\dot\\alpha\\in\\{\\dot1,\\dot2\\}.$$\n\nDefine the nilpotent coordinate shift $\\Delta y^\\mu=i\\sum_{\\alpha,\\dot\\alpha}\\theta^\\alpha\\sigma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\dot\\alpha}\\bar\\theta^{\\dot\\alpha}$ and $y^\\mu=x^\\mu+\\Delta y^\\mu$. For a smooth coefficient function $f$, $f(y)$ means the finite Taylor substitution $\\sum_{k=0}^{2}(\\Delta y^{\\mu_1}\\cdots\\Delta y^{\\mu_k}/k!)\\,\\partial_{\\mu_1}\\cdots\\partial_{\\mu_k}f(x)$, with the $k=0$ term equal to $f(x)$ and repeated spacetime indices summed; higher terms vanish in $\\Lambda_4$. The chiral components are the unique smooth coefficient functions $A,F\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^{1,3},\\mathcal A_{\\bar0})$ and $\\psi_1,\\psi_2\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^{1,3},\\mathcal A_{\\bar1})$ in\n\n$$\\Phi(y,\\theta)=A(y)+\\theta^1\\psi_1(y)+\\theta^2\\psi_2(y)+\\theta^1\\theta^2F(y).$$\n\nThe total parity of every term is even. Define the supersymmetry generators on $\\mathscr S$ by\n\n$$Q_\\alpha=\\partial^L_{\\theta^\\alpha}-i\\sum_{\\mu,\\dot\\alpha}\\sigma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\dot\\alpha}\\bar\\theta^{\\dot\\alpha}\\partial_\\mu,\\qquad \\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha}=-\\partial^L_{\\bar\\theta^{\\dot\\alpha}}+i\\sum_{\\mu,\\alpha}\\theta^\\alpha\\sigma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\dot\\alpha}\\partial_\\mu.$$\n\nFor external odd constant parameters $\\epsilon^\\alpha,\\bar\\epsilon^{\\dot\\alpha}$ that anticommute with the odd generators and coefficient algebra, set $\\delta_{\\epsilon,\\bar\\epsilon}\\Phi=\\sum_\\alpha\\epsilon^\\alpha Q_\\alpha\\Phi+\\sum_{\\dot\\alpha}\\bar\\epsilon^{\\dot\\alpha}\\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha}\\Phi$. The component transformation is, by definition, the unique ordered-basis coefficient tuple of this transformed superfield in the displayed $y,\\theta$ expansion. The space of fields is classical and smooth, so these differential operations have common domain $\\mathscr S$; no quantum-field operator domain or field equation is assumed. In natural units $[x^\\mu]=-1$ and $[\\theta]=[\\bar\\theta]=-\\tfrac12$. If $[\\Phi]=d$ is assigned, the formal component dimensions are $[A]=d$, $[\\psi_\\alpha]=d+\\tfrac12$, and $[F]=d+1$; no value of $d$ is fixed. In the same natural-unit convention, $D_\\alpha$, $\\bar D_{\\dot\\alpha}$, $Q_\\alpha$ and $\\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha}$ have mass dimension $+\\tfrac12$, while $[\\epsilon^\\alpha]=[\\bar\\epsilon^{\\dot\\alpha}]=-\\tfrac12$, so $\\delta_{\\epsilon,\\bar\\epsilon}$ is dimensionless.",
      "uses": [
        "1.1"
      ]
