+          "id": "step-1-1",
+          "claim": "The three nonzero elements of $\\mathcal C$ are $(1,0)$, $(0,1)$, and $(1,1)$. Each generates an order-two subgroup with the origin. The pairing is alternating, so each such subgroup is isotropic. Any subgroup containing two distinct nonzero elements contains two linearly independent columns in $\\mathbb Z_2^2$; the determinant $em'-me'$ of two independent columns is nonzero and therefore equals $1$ in $\\mathbb Z_2$. Hence such a subgroup is not isotropic. Therefore these are exactly the three maximal isotropic subgroups, and [F2] gives their source-reported gauge-group labels.",
