+          "id": "step-1-1",
+          "claim": "For $\\lambda>0$, define $K_\\lambda=(Q+S)|_{\\mathcal H_\\lambda}$. Since $Q^2=S^2=0$ and $\\delta=2(SQ+QS)$, $K_\\lambda$ is an odd self-adjoint operator and $K_\\lambda^2=\\lambda I/2$. Hence $B_\\lambda=\\sqrt{2/\\lambda}\\,K_\\lambda$ is an odd involution on the finite-dimensional eigenspace: it maps $\\mathcal H_\\lambda^{\\bar0}$ bijectively to $\\mathcal H_\\lambda^{\\bar1}$ and conversely.",
+          "step": "1.1",
+          "inputs": [
+            "given",
+            "F1",
+            "A1",
+            "algebra"
+          ]
+        },
+        {
+          "id": "step-2-1",
+          "claim": "The operator $U$ commutes with $K_\\lambda$, so the two parity subspaces have equal $U$-traces under the bijection $B_\\lambda$. Therefore $\\operatorname{Tr}_{\\mathcal H_\\lambda}((-1)^F U)=0$ for every $\\lambda>0$. Trace class in [A1] permits the eigenspace traces to be summed; only $\\mathcal H_0=\\ker\\delta$ contributes, where $e^{-\\beta\\delta}=I$.",
+          "step": "2.1",
+          "inputs": [
+            "given",
+            "F1",
+            "A1",
+            "step 1.1",
+            "algebra"
+          ]
+        },
+        {
+          "id": "step-3-1",
+          "claim": "For $\\psi\\in\\ker\\delta$, positivity and $\\delta=2(SQ+QS)$ give $0=\\langle\\psi,\\delta\\psi\\rangle=2(\\|Q\\psi\\|^2+\\|S\\psi\\|^2)$, so $Q\\psi=S\\psi=0$. Conversely, $Q\\psi=S\\psi=0$ implies $\\delta\\psi=0$. Thus $\\ker\\delta=\\ker Q\\cap\\ker S$, and the remaining trace is exactly the stated trace on that joint kernel.",
+          "step": "3.1",
