se are the two scalar-primary positivity bounds used on this page.\n\nLet $J_1',\\ldots,J_k'$ be mutually commuting self-adjoint charges that commute with $Q$ and $S$ and preserve fermion parity. For $\\beta>0$ and chemical potentials $\\boldsymbol\\mu=(\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_k)$ such that the weighted heat operator is trace class, define the index observable\n\n$$\\mathcal I(\\beta,\\boldsymbol\\mu)=\\operatorname{Tr}_{\\mathcal H(S^3)}\\left((-1)^F\\exp\\left[-\\beta\\delta+\\sum_{a=1}^k\\mu_aJ_a'\\right]\\right).$$\n\nIf the trace is not trace class, the same symbol may be used only for a specified formal series whose coefficients are defined; it does not denote a convergent partition function. The index is called protected against $\\beta$-variation when the stated operator and trace hypotheses imply that positive-$\\delta$ states cancel in the graded trace, as proved by the index-pair theorem on this page. A source's further claim of invariance under changing a coupling requires its own stated deformation hypotheses. A protected local operator means a named primary whose dimension is fixed by a specified shortening relation; the word alone asserts no deformation invariance.\n\nFor the quantum coupling family, use the source normalization\n\n$$\\tau=\\frac{\\theta}{2\\pi}+\\frac{4\\pi i}{g^2},\\qquad \\operatorname{Im}\\tau>0,$$\n\nwhere, on a closed oriented Euclidean four-manifold $X$ with an $SU(2)$ bundle, $k=-\\frac1{8\\pi^2}\\int_X\\operatorname{Tr}_2(F_A\\wedge F_A)\\in\\mathbb Z$ is the instanton number in the selected trace convention and $\\theta$ weights its sectors by $e^{i\\theta k}$. The corresponding source-reported $S$ and $T$ parameter maps are $S:\\tau\\mapsto-1/\\tau$ and $T:\\tau\\mapsto\\tau+1$. The global form and line-operator spectrum are part of the theory data, not determined by the Lie algebra alone. In the $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ center-charge sector define $\\mathcal C=\\mathbb Z_2\\times\\mathbb Z_2$ with charge $(e,m)$ and mutual-locality